ans is after you are admitted you will  get information about the financial aid  process from our financial aid office  and you will submit documents talking to  us and helping us to understand a little  bit more about your assets and your  income and the level of fellowships that  you will receive will be based on that  information so whether you are a  domestic student or you are an  international student you are eligible  for fellowships we will also talk to you  about loan options as well the other  question is tangentially from financial  aid is talking a little bit about the  youth Stafford USA mba fellowship this  is a brand new fellowship that we have  launched this year and with this  fellowship it is specifically targeting  students that have very strong  demonstrated ties to the 12 states in  the United States that make up the  Midwest region so we have a ton of  information about this on the website we  also have a recording of a previous  webinar we did about the fellowship so  if you have any further questions you  can ask about that  the specific question here is what are  we looking for in a candidate so with  this process you will apply to the  fellowship within the MBA application so  we're looking for at a baseline the  exact same thing we're looking for in  the general MBA application so the  intellectual vitality demonstrated  leadership potential and the personal  qualities and contributions in addition  to that we are looking for you to help  us understand what your ties are to the  region and also the impact that we want  that you want to have upon your return  to to one of those 12 states if you are  interested in the fellowship you must  apply by the round 2 deadline this year  and again for more information  definitely check out our website another  question back to our students is what  has been your favorite experience at  Stanford so far let's start with that  one start tell me good  I guess the highlight is in essence what  I'm taking from that question for me I  was giving a talk talk as a tradition we  have at the GSB every Wednesday night  two students come up and give a talk on  their life the origin actually was I  just found out it was basically someone  saying oh you know there's so many  incredible people here and we don't  really know that much about them because  everyone is so humble and Andy Dunn was  at GSB Nate was like well then why don't  you talk about it and so it really was  just a chance to talk about who you are  and I was fortunate to give I think the  third talk of the year which was  basically talking to a bunch of people I  didn't know yet and from that was  incredible the number of emails and kind  words and people reaching out to really  get to know me and so it it made me very  feel very safe and the community and  also very respected and valued yeah  favorite experience I give a talk as  well and I agree with you Talia that is  an extremely rewarding experience to  show you story with your classmates for  me my highlight however was the GSB show  which is a musical that is entirely  written and directed and produced by GSB  students and it's a comedy you can say  I'm kind of making fun of ourselves here  at the GSB and that for me was a  highlight because not only because of  all the effort that went into it but  also because of this really cool  opportunity to be on stage with your  classmates and and entertain 1,200  professors fellow classmates significant  others and so on and so forth yeah yeah  so so here the funny thing is I was  actually only acting but they gave us  microphones so I had a microphone on my  on my ears or in my face all the time  and I was dancing and why we were  dancing some people in the front were  singing so everybody thought I was also  singing so everybody was kind of  congratulating me on how great of a  singer I am they didn't know that the  microphone was actually offered while I  was dancing but that was personal thing  so okay so question about what is the  best way to prepare to get the most out  of Stanford  for those who are able to be admitted to  the program I asked myself this a lot  still asking myself this it's time as  quickly ticking away on this incredible  experience I think feedback I got that  was super helpful was to spend time with  your loved ones  I cannot underestimate how intensive an  experience is and how little time you  will have to spend with friends and  family outside of this world not because  you don't want to but because your day  it's just incredibly full so really if  you can take the summer or the year  before to spend time with people you  love and give things to prioritize  things will change as we've discussed  but if you can have a clearer sense of  what you're here to get it'll be that  much easier to make the decisions that  flow in almost on an hourly basis there  are so many opportunities with every  hour of your day here and without a  clearer sense of what your priorities  are it's really easy to get swept away  into things that are really cool and  really valuable but maybe not what  you're here to get yeah I really wanna  echo that in and I kind of mentioned  that earlier as the North Star I feel  you should if you come to school you  should have a general idea you should  have a really good idea of who you are  what your values are and kind of stay  true to them throughout the experience  as I said I think there's a lot of  there's social pressures there's social  influence there's a lot of things you're  exposed to that kind of shape your  thinking and if they can really drag you  into all different directions I feel and  but if you know what you came here for  and what you want to get out of it and  if as Talley said if you know your  priorities in advance that that really  helps make does it make good decisions  and and be happy with the decisions you  make great okay well we are coming up on  45 minutes but before we go just sold a  couple last things almost tied to the  previous question any final words of  advice for people who are still trying  to figure out whether an MBA is right  for them yeah yeah it's a tricky  question I there is no right answer I  don't think there is no there's no right  applicant a right student for an MBA I  think an MBA is such a diverse program  in such a diverse opportunity that  people from all different walks of life  can get something out of it  i i once someone once told me you know  you get an mba if you want to lead if  you want to be the executive of a large  organization and and i had that in the  back of my mind when i came to the GSB  but I think at the GSB I realized that  it's not true for me personally an MBA  is an opportunity to challenge my  thinking to make amazing new friends to  take classes that push me and really  also like just have some intellectual  kind of freedom to to pursue things that  I at my time at Google would have never  considered so just one quick example I  was working on developing an investment  thesis for Iran and I wanted to pursue  that over the summer and I worked with  it on a perfect with a professor but  that's something I would have never even  considered while I was at Google but at  the GSB you're exposed to so many great  things that it makes it possible point  being I think an MBA gives you a lot of  opportunities it really comes down to  you if you want to take advantage of  those or not I think that's great the  only thing I will add is in terms of  like whether or not it's right or when  it's right really just try to figure out  who you are and I say that because  that's a huge part of the application  process it's also a huge part of what we  do here I would say more than half my  time is spent on reflection and feedback  to better understand what my strengths  are and what my values are and then to  improve upon those that comes in to play  very strongly in the application as well  so as much time as you have given to  making that decision I would say  personally I allocated a lot of that  time towards just figuring out who I was  and what I wanted and from that I think  naturally decision hopefully will flow  and in terms of timing I wasn't I  started my application in October for  round two  I thought oh I should just wait a year  and forget it and then I thought you  know who cares like I'm just gonna give  it a try and it worked out so not saying  that's the right path but certainly  don't limit yourself just because you  don't think it's the right time for  external pressures great well thank you  so much tally it will need for joining  us and for all of you around the world  for watching you can learn more about  the MBA program on our website and by  watching our videos on YouTube
 

